        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "743762c6-7080-5025-89a8-e77f578c20a4",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Products: Black Book of the Admiralty\n- Organizations: High Court of Admiralty\n- People: Eleanor of Aquitaine, Edward I of England, Edward III of England\n- Systems: Rolls of Oléron, Liber memorandorum\n- Concepts: English admiralty law\n\nFacts:\n- Eleanor of Aquitaine promulgated the Rolls of Oléron around 1160.\n- Edward I of England lived from 17 or 18 June 1239 to 7 July 1307 and was King of England from 1272 to 1307.\n- The Black Book of the Admiralty states that the High Court of Admiralty was established during the reign of Edward I of England.\n- More recent scholarship places the establishment of the High Court of Admiralty around 1360 during the reign of Edward III of England.\n- The Liber memorandorum dates to 1338 and a separate manuscript copy is held in the archives of the City of London.\n- The Black Book of the Admiralty is a compilation of English admiralty law created across several English monarchs’ reigns and includes important decisions of the High Court of Admiralty.\n- The Black Book of the Admiralty begins with the Rolls of Oléron, but the Black Book itself was compiled later than those rolls.\n- The earliest statute referred to in the Black Book of the Admiralty apart from the Rolls of Oléron is the Liber memorandorum.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "e18d3d73-3a68-52d9-91d3-f560440bfc2d",
        "text": "Page: Black Book of the Admiralty. Sentence: The Black Book of the Admiralty is a compilation of English admiralty law created over the course of several English monarchs' reigns, including the most important decisions of the High Court of Admiralty.\nPage: Black Book of the Admiralty. Sentence: Its starting point is the Rolls of Oléron, which were promulgated in c. 1160 by Eleanor of Aquitaine, although the \"Black Book\" is undoubtedly later.\nPage: Black Book of the Admiralty. Sentence: The book itself states that the High Court of Admiralty was established during the reign of Edward I (1272–1307), although more recent scholarship places the establishment at c. 1360 during the reign of Edward III.\nPage: Black Book of the Admiralty. Sentence: Apart from the Rolls of Oléron, the earliest statute referred to is the \"Liber memorandorum\" (1338), of which a separate manuscript copy is available in the archives of the City of London.\nPage: Edward I of England. Sentence: Edward I (17/18 June 1239 – 7 July 1307), also known as Edward Longshanks and the Hammer of the Scots (Latin: \"Malleus Scotorum\" ), was King of England from 1272 to 1307.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
      },
